t week; Specifically, the label-distribution-aware margin loss is combined with the error-rate-based cross-entropy loss via combination factor, which considers both imbalanced data representation learning and classifier overall performance. ... Therefore, the study of classification methods suitable for imbalanced hyperspectral images is of crucial theoretical and practical value. ... In addition, for deep learning models show excellent performance in hyperspectral image classification in recent years [14], we have focused on some research on training methods for imbalanced deep learning models.\n\nVolume 18, Issue 10 pp. 2553-2567\n\nORIGINAL RESEARCH\n\nOpen Access\n\n# Factor annealing decoupling compositional training method for imbalanced hyperspectral image classification\n\nFirst published: 01 May 2024\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1049/ipr2.13116Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\nview metrics\n\nXiaojun Li and Yi Su contributed equally to this work.\n\n## Abstract\n\nDue to differences in the quantity and size of observed targets, hyperspectral images are characterized by class imbalance. The standard deep learning classification model training scheme optimizes the overall classification error, which may lead to performance imbalance between classes in hyperspectral image classification frameworks. Therefore, a novel factor annealing decoupling compositional training method is proposed in this paper. Without requiring resampling or reweighting, it implicitly modulates the training process, so standard models can sufficiently learn the representation of the minority classes and further be trained as robust classifiers. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Alignment of Hyperspectral Images Using KAZE Features (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325161026_Alignment_of_Hyperspectral_Images_Using_KAZE_Features)\nciteturn156search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; The scale space is b